<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Where Should I Start?</h2>
<p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">If you are new to acupressure, a simple path works well:</p>
<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">
<li>Read <a href="/basics/what-is-acupressure" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">What Is Acupressure?</a> for a short overview of the practice and how it differs from acupuncture.</li>
<li>Read <a href="/techniques/how-to-do-acupressure" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">How to Do Acupressure at Home</a> to learn the core steps: locate the point, press at a 90-degree angle, build pressure over 10-15 seconds, and hold for 30 seconds to 3 minutes while breathing deeply. You are aiming for a deep, dull ache (deqi), never sharp pain.</li>
<li>Start with a few beginner-friendly points such as <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4 (Hegu)</a> for headaches and pain, <a href="/points/pc6-neiguan-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">PC6 (Neiguan)</a> for nausea and anxiety, and <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">ST36 (Zusanli)</a> for digestion and energy.</li>
<li>Once you are comfortable, browse the condition guides for complete protocols and the body regions for points organized by location.</li>
<li>Before practicing — especially during pregnancy or with a chronic condition — review the <a href="/safety/who-should-avoid-acupressure" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">Safety</a> section.</li>
</ol>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900 mb-3">
<summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is acupressure safe?</summary>
<div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Acupressure is generally safe when done gently — aim for a deep, dull ache rather than sharp pain, and avoid broken or irritated skin. Certain points, including LI4 (Hegu), SP6 (Sanyinjiao), BL60 (Kunlun), and GB21 (Jianjing), are traditionally avoided during pregnancy. Check with a healthcare provider if you are pregnant, take blood-thinning medication, or have a chronic medical condition.</div>
</details>
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900 mb-3">
<summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press each point?</summary>
<div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Standard practice is 30 seconds to 3 minutes per point. Use 30-60 seconds for facial points, sensitive areas, or acute symptoms; 1-2 minutes for most points on the arms, legs, and torso; and up to 2-3 minutes for deeper points on larger muscles.</div>
</details>
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
<summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How often can I do acupressure?</summary>
<div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For acute symptoms such as a sudden headache, repeat every 30-60 minutes as needed. For chronic conditions, practice once or twice daily — consistency over weeks produces the most benefit. For general wellness, three to four times a week is enough; a full session typically covers 4-6 points and lasts 10-20 minutes.</div>
</details>
</section></div>
